    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Regular polynomial field functional algebra",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#regular-polynomial-field-functional-algebra",
        "design_state": "Finite symmetric compact coefficient families define the regular algebra and its smooth functional derivative kernels; local diagonal distributions are excluded.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
          "def-test-function-topology",
          "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables",
          "thm-sequential-convergence-in-test-function-space",
          "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable",
          "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles",
          "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-test-function-topology": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-sequential-convergence-in-test-function-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
        "statement": "Let \\(d\\ge1\\), \\(X=\\mathbb R^d\\), and \\(n\\ge1\\). Write\n\\(\\mathcal D(X^n)=C_c^\\infty(X^n;\\mathbb C)\\) for the complex test-function\nspace of smooth compactly supported functions\n([[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]), with its LF topology\n([[def-test-function-topology]]). The convergence criterion used below is\nthat convergent test sequences have one common compact support and converge\nuniformly with every derivative\n([[thm-sequential-convergence-in-test-function-space]]). Here \\(C^\\infty\\)\nmeans every coordinate derivative exists and is continuous\n([[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]). All integrals are\ncompact Riemann integrals: each integrand is continuous with compact support,\nso restrict it to a compact rectangle and apply\n[[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]]. Let\n\\(\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)\\) be the subspace of kernels invariant under\nevery permutation of their \\(n\\) \\(X\\)-variables. Fields are real smooth\nconfigurations \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty(X;\\mathbb R)\\). For an integer \\(N\\ge0\\)\nand a finite family\n\\(f_0\\in\\mathbb C\\), \\(f_n\\in\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)\\) for\n\\(1\\le n\\le N\\), define\n\\[\nF_f(\\phi)=f_0+\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\int_{X^n}f_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\prod_{j=1}^{n}\\phi(x_j)\\,dx_1\\cdots dx_n.\n\\]\nThe pairing is complex-bilinear, with no conjugation on the kernel or field.\nAn element of \\(\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)\\) is this finite symmetric\ncoefficient family, evaluated on fields by \\(F_f\\). Pointwise sums, scalar\nmultiples, and products again have finite symmetric compactly supported\ncoefficient families: for products, expand the two finite sums and\nsymmetrize each tensor product \\(f_m\\otimes g_n\\). Thus these elements form\nan algebra; the product-integral identity follows from Riemann Fubini on a\nrectangle containing the compact supports\n([[thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles]]).\n\nFor \\(k=0\\), set \\(F_f^{(0)}(\\phi)=F_f(\\phi)\\). For \\(1\\le k\\le N\\),\nthe \\(k\\)-th functional derivative kernel assigned to this coefficient\nfamily is\n\\[\nF_f^{(k)}(\\phi)(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)=\\sum_{n=k}^{N}\\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}\\int_{X^{n-k}}f_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\prod_{j=k+1}^{n}\\phi(x_j)\\,dx_{k+1}\\cdots dx_n.\n\\]\nFor \\(k>N\\) it is zero. Each displayed kernel is smooth, symmetric and compactly supported in its \\(k\\) remaining variables: compactness follows by projection of the finite union of coefficient-kernel supports, and derivatives pass through the finite-compact integrals by [[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle]].\nIn the term \\(n=k\\), the empty integral and empty product mean simply\n\\(f_k(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)\\).\n\nThe scope is exactly smooth compact coefficient kernels. A local functional\nwhose higher derivative kernels are distributions on a thin diagonal is not\nincluded merely because its field expression is polynomial.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Klaus Fredenhagen and Katarzyna Rejzner, Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory, complete lecture notes, section 4",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1208.1428",
              "locator": "Complete 42-page notes, section 4, equation (23) and the discussion of regular polynomial and local functionals."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-qft-diagonal-delta-distribution",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "The diagonal delta is a distribution supported on the diagonal",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#regular-polynomial-field-functional-algebra",
        "design_state": "Authorized local A-page prerequisite: defines the diagonal delta by its test pairing and proves continuity, exact support, symmetry, and the smooth-kernel obstruction used by the B remark.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
          "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables",
          "def-test-function-topology",
          "def-distribution",
          "def-support-of-a-distribution",
          "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-test-function-topology": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-support-of-a-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Let \\(X=\\mathbb R^4\\) and let \\(\\Delta_X=\\{(x,y)\\in X\\times X:x=y\\}\\).\nFor \\(\\Psi\\in\\mathcal D(X\\times X)\\), define\n\\[\n\\langle\\delta_{\\Delta_X},\\Psi\\rangle\n=\\int_X\\Psi(x,x)\\,d^4x.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\delta_{\\Delta_X}\\in\\mathcal D'(X\\times X)\\) is a continuous\ncomplex-linear distribution with support exactly \\(\\Delta_X\\), and it is\nsymmetric under exchanging \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). No nonzero smooth function on\n\\(X\\times X\\) is supported on \\(\\Delta_X\\).",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Klaus Fredenhagen and Katarzyna Rejzner, Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory, complete lecture notes, section 4",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1208.1428",
              "locator": "Complete 42-page notes, section 4, the local-functional discussion and diagonal-supported derivative kernels; the continuity proof below is supplied locally."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-qft-bounded-regulator-dyson-factorization",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Bounded regulated Dyson convergence and temporal factorization",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#regular-polynomial-field-functional-algebra",
        "design_state": "Proves norm convergence, uniqueness, unitarity, composition, and separated-time factorization for bounded strongly continuous self-adjoint operators.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-hilbert-space",
          "def-space-of-bounded-linear-operators",
          "def-operator-norm",
          "def-hilbert-space-adjoint",
          "thm-extreme-value-metric",
          "def-complex-exponential",
          "lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere",
          "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable",
          "thm-ftc-second-part",
          "thm-algebra-of-derivatives"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-space-of-bounded-linear-operators": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-operator-norm": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-hilbert-space-adjoint": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-extreme-value-metric": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-complex-exponential": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let \\(\\mathcal H\\) be a complex Hilbert space\n([[def-hilbert-space]]), \\(I=[a,b]\\) with \\(a\\le b\\),\nand \\(V:I\\to\\mathcal B(\\mathcal H)\\) a strongly continuous family of\nself-adjoint bounded operators (self-adjoint means \\(V(t)=V(t)^*\\), using the\nHilbert adjoint of [[def-hilbert-space-adjoint]]) with\n\\(M:=\\sup_{t\\in I}\\|V(t)\\|<\\infty\\). Strong continuity means that\n\\(t\\mapsto V(t)\\psi\\) is norm-continuous for every \\(\\psi\\in\\mathcal H\\).\nDefine \\(U_0(t,s)=I_{\\mathcal H}\\) and recursively\n\\[\nU_{n+1}(t,s)\\psi=-i\\int_s^t V(r)U_n(r,s)\\psi\\,dr,\\qquad a\\le s,t\\le b,\n\\]\nwhere the integral is the vector-valued Riemann integral and is oriented when\n\\(t<s\\). Then\n\\[\n\\|U_0(t,s)\\|\\le1,\\qquad\n\\|U_n(t,s)\\|\\le \\frac{M^n|t-s|^n}{n!}\\quad(n\\ge1).\n\\]\nFor every complex \\(z\\), \\(U_z(t,s)=\\sum_{n\\ge0}z^nU_n(t,s)\\) converges in\noperator norm, uniformly on \\(I^2\\), and is the unique strongly continuous\nsolution of\n\\[\nU_z(t,s)=I_{\\mathcal H}-iz\\int_s^t V(r)U_z(r,s)\\,dr .\n\\]\nFor real \\(z\\), \\(U_z(t,s)\\) is unitary and obeys\n\\(U_z(t,r)U_z(r,s)=U_z(t,s)\\). If \\(V_2\\) vanishes outside \\([a,c]\\) and\n\\(V_1\\) vanishes outside \\([d,b]\\), where \\(c<d\\), then\n\\[\nU_z^{V_1+V_2}(b,a)=U_z^{V_1}(b,a)U_z^{V_2}(b,a),\n\\]\nwith the later-time factor on the left, provided \\(a\\le c<d\\le b\\). This\nidentity also holds\ncoefficientwise as a formal power series in \\(z\\).",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, complete author-hosted Cambridge lecture notes, chapter 3",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S3.html",
              "locator": "Complete HTML chapter 3, section 3.1.1, equations (3.221)–(3.225), the time-ordered Dyson series. Its displayed argument is formal; the norm bounds and strong-continuity proof here are supplied locally."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-tensor-test-function-density",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Tensor-product tests are dense in smooth compact kernel topology",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#regular-polynomial-field-functional-algebra",
        "design_state": "Proves tensor-test density in the LF topology and coefficient-representation uniqueness by periodic Fourier truncation and product cutoffs.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra",
          "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
          "def-test-function-topology",
          "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables",
          "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization",
          "cor-compact-domain-maps-are-uniformly-continuous",
          "thm-sequential-convergence-in-test-function-space",
          "def-complex-exponential",
          "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension",
          "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself",
          "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
          "thm-fejer-uniform-convergence-for-continuous-periodic-functions",
          "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles",
          "thm-multidimensional-integral-properties",
          "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable",
          "thm-ftc-second-part",
          "thm-algebra-of-derivatives"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-test-function-topology": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization": "mathematical-premise",
          "cor-compact-domain-maps-are-uniformly-continuous": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-sequential-convergence-in-test-function-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-complex-exponential": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself": "mathematical-premise",
          "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-fejer-uniform-convergence-for-continuous-periodic-functions": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-multidimensional-integral-properties": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Let \\(d,n\\ge1\\), \\(X=\\mathbb R^d\\), and give\n\\(\\mathcal D(X^n)=C_c^\\infty(X^n;\\mathbb C)\\) its LF test-function topology.\nThe finite linear span of elementary product tests\n\\[\n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\longmapsto\\prod_{j=1}^{n}g_j(x_j),\\qquad g_j\\in\\mathcal D(X),\n\\]\nis dense. More precisely, for each \\(\\Phi\\in\\mathcal D(X^n)\\), there is a\nsequence of such finite sums whose supports lie in one fixed compact product\nand whose derivatives of every multi-index converge uniformly to those of\n\\(\\Phi\\). Symmetrized finite sums are dense in\n\\(\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)\\). Consequently the symmetric-kernel\nrepresentation in [[def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra]] is\nunique: if a finite symmetric-kernel polynomial vanishes on every real smooth\nfield, every coefficient kernel vanishes.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Klaus Fredenhagen and Katarzyna Rejzner, Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory, complete lecture notes, section 4",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1208.1428",
              "locator": "Complete 42-page notes, section 4, equation (23); the Fourier-density and uniqueness argument below is supplied locally."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-qft-regular-wick-star-associativity",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Regular Wick-star contractions are well defined and associative",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#regular-polynomial-field-functional-algebra",
        "design_state": "Defines distributional Wick contractions for continuous bidistributions and proves closure, associativity, the involution rule, and the generator commutator.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra",
          "lem-qft-tensor-test-function-density",
          "thm-tensor-product-distributions-and-iterated-pairings",
          "lem-distribution-pairing-with-smooth-parameter-families"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-tensor-test-function-density": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tensor-product-distributions-and-iterated-pairings": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-distribution-pairing-with-smooth-parameter-families": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Let \\(X=\\mathbb R^d\\), \\(d\\ge1\\), and let \\(W\\in\\mathcal D'(X\\times X)\\) be\nany continuous complex bidistribution. For a real formal parameter \\(\\eta\\)\nand \\(F,G\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)\\), define\n\\[\nF\\star_W G=\\sum_{k\\ge0}\\frac{\\eta^k}{k!}\\left\\langle W^{\\otimes k},F^{(k)}(\\phi)\\otimes G^{(k)}(\\phi)\\right\\rangle .\n\\]\nThe pairing is over \\(k\\) independent pairs of variables; \\(W^{\\otimes k}\\)\nis formed by iterated distributional tensor products, not by multiplying\ndistributions at coincident points. The sum is finite because the inputs are\npolynomial. It defines an element of\n\\(\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)[\\eta]\\), and \\(\\star_W\\) is associative.\nIf \\(W\\) is Hermitian in the sense\n\\(\\overline{\\langle W,\\overline g\\otimes\\overline f\\rangle}\n=\\langle W,f\\otimes g\\rangle\\), then\n\\((F\\star_W G)^*=G^*\\star_W F^*\\), where \\(F^*(\\phi)=\\overline{F(\\phi)}\\).\nFor \\(\\ell_f(\\phi)=\\int_X f(x)\\phi(x)\\,dx\\),\n\\[\n[\\ell_f,\\ell_g]_{\\star_W}=\\eta\\bigl(W(f,g)-W(g,f)\\bigr),\n\\qquad W(f,g):=\\langle W,f\\otimes g\\rangle .\n\\]",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Klaus Fredenhagen and Katarzyna Rejzner, Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory, complete lecture notes, section 5",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1208.1428",
              "locator": "Complete 42-page notes, section 5, equations (56)–(64), the regular-functional star product and the change-of-kernel normal-ordering map."
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, complete author-hosted Cambridge lecture notes, chapter 3",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S3.html",
              "locator": "Complete HTML chapter 3, sections 3.3.1–3.3.2, equations (3.235)–(3.245), contraction and Wick expansion. The distributional and functional-domain arguments below are proved locally."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-regular-normal-order-isomorphism",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Regular normal-ordering change is an exact star isomorphism",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#regular-polynomial-field-functional-algebra",
        "design_state": "Proves the finite second-derivative exponential gives an exact star-algebra isomorphism for a symmetric distributional kernel shift.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra",
          "lem-qft-tensor-test-function-density",
          "thm-qft-regular-wick-star-associativity",
          "thm-tensor-product-distributions-and-iterated-pairings",
          "lem-distribution-pairing-with-smooth-parameter-families"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-tensor-test-function-density": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-qft-regular-wick-star-associativity": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tensor-product-distributions-and-iterated-pairings": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-distribution-pairing-with-smooth-parameter-families": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Let \\(W\\in\\mathcal D'(X\\times X)\\), \\(X=\\mathbb R^d\\), and let\n\\(K\\in\\mathcal D'(X\\times X)\\) be symmetric under interchange of its\nvariables. Define\n\\[\n\\Gamma_KF(\\phi)=\\langle K,F^{(2)}(\\phi)\\rangle,\\qquad\n\\alpha_K=\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{\\eta}{2}\\Gamma_K\\right)\n=\\sum_{j\\ge0}\\frac{(\\eta/2)^j}{j!}\\Gamma_K^j\n\\]\non \\(\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)[\\eta]\\). Each sum is finite on each\npolynomial because \\(\\Gamma_K\\) lowers degree by two. Then \\(\\alpha_K\\) is\ninvertible with inverse \\(\\alpha_{-K}\\), and\n\\[\n\\alpha_K(F\\star_W G)=\\alpha_K(F)\\star_{W+K}\\alpha_K(G)\n\\]\nfor all regular polynomial functionals \\(F,G\\). Thus the change from \\(W\\)\nto \\(W+K\\) is an exact algebra isomorphism on this regular class.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Klaus Fredenhagen and Katarzyna Rejzner, Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory, complete lecture notes, section 5",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1208.1428",
              "locator": "Complete 42-page notes, section 5, equations (63)–(64) and the statement that the kernel-change map intertwines the regular star products."
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, complete author-hosted Cambridge lecture notes, chapter 3",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S3.html",
              "locator": "Complete HTML chapter 3, section 3.3.2, equations (3.243)–(3.245), normal ordering and contractions; the distributional isomorphism is proved locally."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "def-qft-regular-formal-time-ordering",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Regular time-ordered contraction coefficients",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#regular-polynomial-field-functional-algebra",
        "design_state": "Defines the translation-invariant bidistribution lift, causal support order, finite time-ordered contractions, and the formal regular S-series.",
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra",
          "lem-qft-tensor-test-function-density",
          "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
          "def-test-function-topology",
          "def-distribution",
          "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
          "def-schwartz-topology-and-convergence",
          "def-tempered-distribution",
          "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable",
          "thm-tensor-product-distributions-and-iterated-pairings",
