        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-tensor-test-function-density": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-qft-diagonal-delta-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-test-function-topology": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-support-of-a-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-qft-regular-wick-star-associativity": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-regular-formal-time-ordering": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "For a nonzero real $g\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)$, the local quartic\nfunctional $L_g(\\phi)=\\int g(x)\\phi(x)^4\\,dx$ is not in the regular\npolynomial class of [[def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra]]. Write\n$\\delta(x-y)$ for the distribution\n$\\delta_{\\Delta_X}$ defined in\n[[thm-qft-diagonal-delta-distribution]], and define the diagonal kernel\n$K_g$ by\n$$ \\langle K_g,\\Psi\\rangle =12\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}g(x)\\Psi(x,x)\\,d^4x. $$\nThe local quartic's second derivative at $\\phi\\equiv1$ has this kernel.\nThe verification below shows it is nonzero and cannot be a smooth compact\ncoefficient kernel, so no other regular presentation can represent $L_g$.\nThe regular star product in\n[[thm-qft-regular-wick-star-associativity]] and the formal time-ordering\nconstruction in [[def-qft-regular-formal-time-ordering]] take regular polynomial\nfunctionals as inputs. Since $L_g$ is not in that class, neither construction\napplies to it. Extending either operation across the diagonal requires a\nseparately stated and proved construction; no such extension or general\ncausal-renormalization theorem is asserted on this page.\n\nThe nonzero hypothesis matters: if $g=0$, then $L_g=0$ is the regular\nconstant family with all coefficient kernels zero.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Klaus Fredenhagen and Katarzyna Rejzner, Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory, complete lecture notes, sections 4–5",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1208.1428",
              "locator": "Complete 42-page notes, section 4, equations (23)–(25), distinguishing regular from local functionals; section 5.2, the Wick-square extension discussion."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-qft-regular-quadratic-star-product",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Explicit regular quadratic Wick star product",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#regular-polynomial-field-functional-algebra",
        "design_state": "Computes each of the three finite quadratic Wick contraction orders exactly.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra",
          "thm-qft-regular-wick-star-associativity",
          "def-distribution"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-qft-regular-wick-star-associativity": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-distribution": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Let \\(f,g\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^d)\\), let\n\\(\\ell_f(\\phi)=\\int f(x)\\phi(x)\\,dx\\), and let \\(W\\) be any continuous\nbidistribution. For the regular quadratic functionals\n\\(F(\\phi)=\\ell_f(\\phi)^2\\) and \\(G(\\phi)=\\ell_g(\\phi)^2\\), the finite\ncontraction formula is\n\\[\nF\\star_W G\n=\\ell_f^2\\ell_g^2+4\\eta W(f,g)\\ell_f\\ell_g+2\\eta^2W(f,g)^2.\n\\]\nThe formula is exact in the formal parameter \\(\\eta\\).",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Klaus Fredenhagen and Katarzyna Rejzner, Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory, complete lecture notes, section 5",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1208.1428",
              "locator": "Complete 42-page notes, section 5, equation (56) for regular contractions and Example 5.3 for the quadratic Wick-contraction coefficients."
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, complete author-hosted Cambridge lecture notes, chapter 3",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S3.html",
              "locator": "Complete HTML chapter 3, section 3.3.2, equations (3.243)–(3.245), the four-field contraction expansion."
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
